# Proteomics And Metabolomics


@left


- 1. What is proteomics? What is metabolomics? What are they used for?


@top-right


![Student-created workflow from sample collection to protein identification.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_proteomics-workflow.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to proteomics and metabolomics](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_001.png)

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Proteomics


@left

- Proteomics: large-scale protein analysis
- Proteome: proteins produced or modified in one organism or system

@top-right

- mRNA levels as translation proxy
- Post-translational modifications
- Alternative splicing
- Protein complexes
- Degradation rates

@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to proteomics and metabolomics](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_001.png)

=== layout: two-panels


# Metabolomics


@left


![Instructor figure related to proteomics and metabolomics](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_001.png)


@right


- Study of chemical processes involving metabolites.
  - Look for unique chemical fingerprints that specific cellular processes leave behind
- Differences\:
  - Proteomics analyze the set of gene products being produced in the cell.
  - Metabolomics gives an instantaneous snapshot of the physiology of that cell.

