# Mass Spectrometry And Protein Identification


@left


- 4. How do we identify unknown proteins? How does a Mass Spectrometry work? What are the three main components of a mass spectrometer? How is protease digestion used in mass spec?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_042.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_043.png)

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Mass Spectroscopy


@left


- Mass spec (MS) is the science of determining the masses of the molecules comprising a sample of material.
- Mass spectrometers are used to measure masses of positive ions and thus measure atomic and molecular masses.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_043.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_044.png)

=== layout: two-panels


# Instrument


@left


- A mass spec instrument contain 4 modules\:
  - ionizer and ion extractor- convert sample to ions and remove ions from sample into air
  - mass analyzer
  - detector


@right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_041.png)

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# The Ionizer


@left


- The ionizer's job into convert your sample into charged ions.
  - Like in a gel, if the sample has no ions, it will not move in an electric field
- The two most common ionizers are\:
  - MALDI - matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization
  - ESI - electrospray ionization
- Choice of ionizer depends on size of macromolecule you are looking at.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_045.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_046.png)

=== layout: two-panels


# MALDI Mass Spec


@left


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_042.png)


@right


- add a matrix material
- shine UV laser on sample + matrix material
- surface of sample vaporizes and also gains charge

=== layout: two-panels


# Electrospray Ionization


@left


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_043.png)


@right


- Sample is squirted from a charged capillary
- turns the macromolecule being ionized into small droplets
- droplets will then be further broken down into even smaller droplets
  - which creates molecules with attached protons

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Mass Analyzer


@left


- A Mass Analyzer sorts the particles out by mass and charge
- There are 3 major types of mass analyzers\:
  - Magnetic sector or double focusing
  - Quadrupole
  - Time of flight


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_048.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_049.png)

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Mass Detector


@left


- Particles with different masses strike the detector at different points; a particle detector makes a scan to determine the number of particles collected at a given radius R.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_049.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_041.png)

=== layout: big-image



@image


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_044.png)


@caption


Mass Spectrometry And Protein Identification

=== layout: two-panels


# Time of Flight Analyzer


@left


- Mass analysis in time-of-flight (TOF) spectrometry is achieved because ions of different mass-to-charge (m/z) values have different velocities and therefore reach the detector at different times.


@right


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_045.png)

=== layout: big-image



@image


![Instructor figure related to mass spectrometry and protein identification](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_046.png)


@caption


Mass Spectrometry And Protein Identification

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Chromatography + Mass Spec


@left

- LC + MS: separate first; identify by mass
- GC-MS: volatile samples
- LC-MS: liquid samples
- Chromatography improves analysis of complex mixtures

